  "body": "the W3C webappsec WG is working for a while on a so-called suborigin concept (current draft here: https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-suborigins/)\r\n\r\nThe goal of suborigins for now is to isolate web apps that are running on the same origin from each other, to make XSS bugs in one of them not affect others. The assumption here is that not all web apps running on the same origin are of the same quality, and it might not be possible to bring all apps to the same level, so sites might want to sandbox some of the apps.\r\n\r\nThe concept of a suborigin is supposed to extend a regular origin with an arbitrary suborigin namespace that is chosen by the server delivering the main resource. We'd extend same-origin checks to also take the suborigin into account, i.e. (scheme, host, port, suboriginA) != (scheme, host, port, suboriginB).\r\n\r\nSimilarly, we'd add window.suborigin that returns the current suborigin, and postMessage / MessageEvent take the suborigin into account.\r\n\r\nI'll file separate issues for storage, fetch / CORS, permissions, and workers. In this issue, I'd like to kick of the discussion about the proposed change to HTML's security model.\r\n\r\n/cc @devd @mikewest @annevk",
